# POD Combat — System Reference

Single source of truth for the combat minigame's runtime model. Code
files reference sections of this doc instead of re-stating the
contract in their own headers.

If you're reading this for the first time, scan the **Architecture
overview** below, then jump to whatever section the file you opened
points at.

---

## Architecture overview

Group combat uses a **two-team system** with teams of arbitrary
size (no positional cap — only a soft guardrail constant
`pod_combat_max_team_size`, default 50). Each combatant is identified
by a `combat_team` flag (`flag:player` / `flag:enemy`) plus a
`team_position` (int 0+, position 0 = leader). Per-combatant state
lives on `POD_combat_slot` story_cycle scopes — one story per slot,
created at combat init and cleaned up at combat end. The only literal
slot-pointer variables on the combat host (the PC) are the two team
leaders: `pod_combat_pc_slot` (player/0) and
`pod_combat_enemy_leader_slot` (enemy/0). Every non-leader slot is
reached by iterating the `pod_combat_active_slots` variable list and
matching `combat_team` + `team_position`.

A transient **action workspace** (`pod_combat_actor_*` /
`pod_combat_target_*`) holds whichever combatant is acting and
whichever one is the current target. It is **not a persistence
layer** — it is a per-action execution adapter, loaded from / saved
back to the appropriate slot story at each action boundary by
`pod_combat_{load,save}_workspace_effect`. Skill effects read and
write only the workspace, so the same code runs for the
PC, AI coterie, the primary opponent, and knights. Outside the
load/save window, the workspace contents are stale by definition —
canonical per-combatant state lives on the slot story. See
§7 *Action workspace lifecycle invariant* for the load/save contract.

Combat hits three supporting story_cycle types besides slots:
`POD_combat_move` (the move catalogue), `POD_combat_log_entry` (rows
in the rolling log), and `POD_combat_sc_context` (caller-context
snapshot for the post-combat follow-up event). Per-actor "AI move
history" pointers (`pod_combat_ai_last_move`, `_prev_move`,
`_prev2_move`) live as regular story-reference variables on the
acting AI character — not as a story_cycle scope.

**Hit chance formula:** `60 + (own prowess − target prowess) / 2`,
clamped `20-95`.

