## §3 Slot lifecycle

`POD_combat_slot` story type (declared in `POD_story_cycle_combat.txt`)
is the sole data container for per-combatant state. Lifecycle helpers
live in `pod_combat_slot_effects.txt`:

- **Lazy creation.** `pod_combat_set_slot_char_effect` (the universal
  fill helper) creates the slot story on its first call for a given
  slot — there is no separate upfront create pass. Cells the
  duel builder never fills get no story_cycle scope on Charon. The
  duel builder drives the fill order:
  `POD_combat_create_duel_effect` seats the PC at player/0,
  `POD_combat_duel_add_ally_effect` appends each ally at the next
  player position (player/1, /2, … incrementing
  `pod_combat_player_team_size`), and `POD_combat_duel_add_enemy_effect`
  appends each enemy at the next enemy position (the first enemy →
  enemy/0, the primary opponent, then enemy/1, /2, … incrementing
  `pod_combat_enemy_team_size`). Both builders are uncapped — only the
  soft guardrail `pod_combat_max_team_size` bounds them. Stat /
  discipline init is deferred to
  `POD_combat_finalize_duel_effect` (`pod_combat_init_all_slots_effect`),
  which runs once the full roster is known.
- **Mid-combat add (summons / reinforcements).** Combatants can also be
  seated into a LIVE fight, after finalize, via
  `pod_combat_add_ally_midfight_effect` / `pod_combat_add_enemy_midfight_effect`
  (in `pod_combat_summon_effects.txt`). Each reuses finalize's per-slot building
  blocks — `POD_combat_duel_add_*_effect` to append + register the slot, then
  `pod_combat_init_slot_from_character_effect` + `pod_combat_calc_one_hit_chance_effect`
  (vs the opposing leader) on just the new slot — and sets `slot_has_acted = 1`
  so the unit sits out the current round and joins initiative next round-start.
  GUI panels, victory check, and turn loops pick it up automatically (all
  iterate `pod_combat_active_slots`); leaders are never recomputed. The move-side
  entry point is §14b of `POD_execute_combat_move`. Temporary summons are tracked
  in `pod_combat_summoned_chars` and `death_disappearance`-d at combat end by
  `pod_combat_group_cleanup_effect`.
- `pod_combat_clear_slot_stories_effect` runs once at combat end
  from `pod_combat_group_cleanup_effect`. It iterates
  `pod_combat_active_slots` (the only slot list), calls `end_story`
  on each, clears the list, then removes the two leader pointer vars
  `pod_combat_pc_slot` / `pod_combat_enemy_leader_slot`.
  `end_story` takes every slot var with it, so
  no per-field cleanup pass is needed.

**Caller scope:** the combat host (the PC). All saves / list adds
happen on the host; the actual stories live on Charon
(`POD_wraith_01`) like the move catalogue and log entries.

### Walking slots: list iteration vs single-slot lookups

There is one slot-walking list and one single-slot lookup pattern in
this codebase. They are NOT interchangeable — picking the wrong one
leads to either spurious work, missed slots, or stale-story leaks.
The rule:

- **`every_in_list = { variable = pod_combat_active_slots ... }`** —
  the canonical slot iteration. `pod_combat_active_slots` is the only
  slot list. Stories are LAZILY created on first
  `pod_combat_set_slot_char_effect` call and added to this list
  atomically; unfilled (team, position) cells have no story_cycle scope on
  Charon. KO'd-but-once-filled slots
  stay in the list with `slot_alive = 0`, so add `pod_combat_slot_is_alive_trigger = yes`
  to the limit if you only want living combatants. Examples:
  round-end soak revert, victory-check team counts, dispatching to
  the slot whose (combat_team, team_position) matches the current actor, AoE damage
  / heal / buff / debuff iteration, post-combat duel teardown,
  post-combat nonfatal wound cap, tier var cleanup, combat-end
  story teardown. Inside the body the iterator's scope IS the slot
  story, so `var:slot_X` reads and `set_variable = { name = slot_X ... }`
  writes work directly. To reach back to host vars, save the host
  first (`save_scope_as = host` or `save_temporary_scope_as = host`
  for trigger contexts) and reference `scope:host.var:Y`.

- **Single-slot lookups** — for operations where a specific slot is
  intentional. The two team leaders resolve through their literal
  pointer vars `pod_combat_pc_slot` (player/0 = PC) and
  `pod_combat_enemy_leader_slot` (enemy/0 = primary opponent); any
  other named slot is found by iterating `pod_combat_active_slots`
  and matching `combat_team` + `team_position`. The hit-chance
  pair-up walk (every non-leader vs its team leader) is itself an
  `every_in_list` loop over the list.

  All dispatchers — including the slot-init dispatcher
  `pod_combat_init_all_slots_effect` — are now `every_in_list` loops
  over `pod_combat_active_slots` rather than unrolled per-position
  call lists, so they scale to any team size.

The `any_in_list` trigger is the read-only counterpart over
`pod_combat_active_slots`: use it for "does any slot satisfy X?"
questions in trigger context (e.g. "is the targeting requirement
satisfied — does any alive enemy exist?"). Pre-Phase-3 the codebase
carried unrolled OR chains for these; the consolidation pass
replaced them with `any_in_list` predicates.

### Slot creation field defaults

`pod_combat_set_slot_char_effect`'s lazy-create branch initialises
each freshly created slot story with the following defaults, then
proceeds to write `slot_char` and add the story to `pod_combat_active_slots`:

| Field | Default | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `combat_team` | `flag:player` / `flag:enemy` | the slot's team, baked at creation |
| `team_position` | the slot's position within its team (int 0+, 0 = leader) | baked at creation |
| `slot_alive` | 1 | the slot is "occupied" until KO'd |
| `slot_morale` | 0 | overwritten from character by `pod_combat_init_slot_from_character_effect` |
| `slot_prowess` | 0 | same |
| `slot_soak` | 0 | same |
| `slot_energy_used` | 0 | same |
| `slot_has_acted` | 0 | same |

These fields are pure values (no absence-as-signal semantics) so a
default of 0 is safe — a slot's presence is established by its
membership in `pod_combat_active_slots` (the iterator's scope is the
slot story), and readers just read the value.

### Fields NOT pre-initialized (deliberately absence-as-signal)

| Field | Absence semantics |
|---|---|
| `slot_char` | meaningful only as a scope reference; absence = "slot is empty" |
| `slot_base_soak` | gate for `pod_combat_recalc_slot_stats_via_story_effect` (its rebuild path branches on `has_variable = slot_base_soak`; the standalone round-end soak revert was retired since the round-tier revert that runs immediately after already rebuilds `slot_soak` from base + remaining tiers) |
| `slot_hit_chance` | presence means "calculate_all_hit_chances has populated this slot's hit chance"; primarily a load_actor / load_target read for the workspace mirror and the GUI's per-slot display path (the legacy synthesis-fallback SV `pod_combat_slot_hit_chance_value` was removed when the display moved to PODCombatSlotHitDisplay) |
| `slot_base_hit_chance` | gate in `pod_combat_recalc_slot_stats_effect` |
| `slot_initiative` | set during initiative calc (alive slots only); presence distinguishes "filled-and-rolled this round" from "unfilled or pre-calc" |
| `slot_turn_order` | turn rank within the round, stamped by `pod_combat_calc_turn_orders_effect` after initiative is rolled |
| `slot_init_bonus` | presence = active initiative bonus to fold into the next initiative roll |
| `slot_ap_drain` | presence = pending AP drain to apply on the actor's next turn |
| `slot_incorporeal_spirit` | presence = combatant is incorporeal; physical attacks no-op unless the attacker has `slot_can_damage_spirit` |
| `slot_can_damage_spirit` | presence = combatant can damage incorporeal targets |
| `slot_is_guarding` / `_is_dodging` / `_is_stealthed` | stance flags |
| `slot_retaliate_*` | retaliate state |
| `slot_*_tier` | tier vars; `has_variable` detects "no buff active" |

Readers of these fields use per-field `has_variable` checks.

